<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pedro Menéndez de Avilés</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1519">1519</a>–<a href="/years/1574">1574</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pedro Menéndez de Avilés was a Spanish admiral who founded St. Augustine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> in 1565, the oldest continuously inhabited European settlement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. In the same year he destroyed the French Huguenot colony at Fort Caroline and executed most of its men. As governor of Spanish Florida he made alliances and conflicts with peoples such as the Calusa.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pedro-menendez-de-aviles.jpg" alt="Pedro Menéndez de Avilés" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: José Camarón y Boronat (1730-1803), published by Fran(cis)co de Paula Marti in 1791 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pedro_menendez_de_Aviles.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Menéndez was born on 15 February 1519 in Avilés, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>, into an old noble family, one of the younger of the twenty children of Juan Alonso Sánchez de Avilés, who had served in the War of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, and María Alonso y Menéndez Arango. His father died while he was a child. He ran away from the relative charged with his education, later served at sea against French corsairs, and with part of his inheritance built a patache, a small fast vessel, in which he captured two French zabras off the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1554 the Crown named him Captain-General of the Fleet of the Indies, a post the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casa-de-contratacion">Casa de Contratación</a> had controlled, and he commanded the treasure fleet again from 1559, routing it through the Florida Strait to use the Gulf Stream. He is credited as the chief planner of the convoy system, and in 1561 he built twelve escort <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galleon">galleons</a> known as the Twelve Apostles. Casa officials jailed him for alleged smuggling in 1561 and again in 1563, the year his son Juan was lost with the ship La Concepción.</p><p>Alarmed by Fort Caroline, founded by René de Laudonnière in 1564, Philip II signed an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asiento">asiento</a> on 20 March 1565 making Menéndez adelantado of La Florida. He sailed from Cádiz on 28 July with over 1,000 sailors, soldiers and settlers and sighted land on 28 August, the feast of St. Augustine. On 20 September his men attacked Fort Caroline, killing 132 Frenchmen and sparing women and children. Soon after, nearly all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-ribault">Jean Ribault</a>'s shipwrecked men were executed after surrendering, at the inlet later called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matanzas">Matanzas</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>As governor he moved the settlement of St. Augustine in 1566 and 1572 and sent the Juan Pardo expedition inland from Santa Elena. He made peace with the Calusa leader Carlos by marrying his sister, baptised Doña Antonia, but used her as a hostage and dealt with the Calusas' enemies, the Tocobagas, and relations sank into a war that went on into the next century. Named governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> in 1567, he died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/typhus">typhus</a> at Santander on 17 September 1574. The World War II Liberty Ship SS Pedro Menendez was named in his honour.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pedro Menéndez de Avilés” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After René Goulaine de Laudonnière founded Fort Caroline for Huguenot colonists in 1564, King Philip II appointed <b>Pedro Menéndez de Avilés</b> Adelantado of Florida, with a commission to drive out non-Spanish adventurers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-florida">Spanish Florida · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pedro Menéndez de Avilés</b> sighted the Florida coast on the feast of St Augustine and gave the saint's name to the harbour.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-28">28 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1566 the chief known to the Spanish as Carlos met <b>Pedro Menéndez de Avilés</b>, who founded a short-lived Jesuit mission.</blockquote><a href="/state/calusa">Calusa</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1565 Philip II sent <b>Pedro Menéndez de Avilés</b>, who founded St Augustine, captured Fort Caroline and executed Ribault and his shipwrecked men at Matanzas Inlet.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-florida">French Florida</a></li><li><blockquote>When French Huguenots built Fort Caroline in French Florida, Philip II sent <b>Pedro Menéndez de Avilés</b>, who founded St Augustine in 1565 and destroyed the French colony.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-florida">Spanish Florida</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Avilés, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santander-spain">Santander</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.56,-5.92&amp;year=1554" aria-label="Avilés on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4408 619 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pedro Menéndez de Avilés"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4858 889h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4858 889h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.56,-5.92&amp;year=1554">What was here in 1554? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
